{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"d825114cfc9c0077","name":"card_event_guerilla_warfare_school","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/ANZAC/events/card_event_guerilla_warfare_school","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"GUERILLA WARFARE SCHOOL","text":"Costs 1 less if right-most in hand. Retreat a random enemy unit.","flavorText":"The Guerrilla Warfare School at Tidal River trained Australian and New Zealand soldiers for irregular warfare in the Pacific. Its courses taught men how to operate in small commando groups, behind enemy lines, without the support expected by normal infantry. The aim was to raid, scout, sabotage, and disappear before a stronger enemy could react. Training focused on practical tactics. Trainees learned ambush drills, night movement, camouflage, silent patrols, map reading, and how to set up hidden observation posts. They practiced cutting communications, blowing bridges with timed charges, and attacking supply routes. Demolition work used explosives such as gelignite, while close-range fighting involved firearms like Bren guns, Thompson submachine guns, grenades, and mortars. The school also taught survival and self-reliance. Men trained to cross rivers, move through swamps, climb rough ground, forage, and keep operating with little food or equipment. Coastal raids were practiced with collapsible boats, while signals training taught small patrols how to report information without exposing themselves. Graduates took these skills into the Pacific, where Independent Companies and later commando units used them for reconnaissance, raids, ambushes, and harassment operations in places such as Timor, New Guinea, and New Britain.","faction":"Anzac","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_guerilla_warfare_school","title":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_guerilla_warfare_school_title","SourceString":"GUERILLA WARFARE SCHOOL","LocalizedString":"GUERILLA WARFARE SCHOOL"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Anzac","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_guerilla_warfare_school_text","SourceString":"Costs 1 less if right-most in hand. Retreat a random enemy unit.","LocalizedString":"Costs 1 less if right-most in hand. Retreat a random enemy unit."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_guerilla_warfare_school_history","SourceString":"The Guerrilla Warfare School at Tidal River trained Australian and New Zealand soldiers for irregular warfare in the Pacific. Its courses taught men how to operate in small commando groups, behind enemy lines, without the support expected by normal infantry. The aim was to raid, scout, sabotage, and disappear before a stronger enemy could react. Training focused on practical tactics. Trainees learned ambush drills, night movement, camouflage, silent patrols, map reading, and how to set up hidden observation posts. They practiced cutting communications, blowing bridges with timed charges, and attacking supply routes. Demolition work used explosives such as gelignite, while close-range fighting involved firearms like Bren guns, Thompson submachine guns, grenades, and mortars. The school also taught survival and self-reliance. Men trained to cross rivers, move through swamps, climb rough ground, forage, and keep operating with little food or equipment. Coastal raids were practiced with collapsible boats, while signals training taught small patrols how to report information without exposing themselves. Graduates took these skills into the Pacific, where Independent Companies and later commando units used them for reconnaissance, raids, ambushes, and harassment operations in places such as Timor, New Guinea, and New Britain.","LocalizedString":"The Guerrilla Warfare School at Tidal River trained Australian and New Zealand soldiers for irregular warfare in the Pacific. Its courses taught men how to operate in small commando groups, behind enemy lines, without the support expected by normal infantry. The aim was to raid, scout, sabotage, and disappear before a stronger enemy could react. Training focused on practical tactics. Trainees learned ambush drills, night movement, camouflage, silent patrols, map reading, and how to set up hidden observation posts. They practiced cutting communications, blowing bridges with timed charges, and attacking supply routes. Demolition work used explosives such as gelignite, while close-range fighting involved firearms like Bren guns, Thompson submachine guns, grenades, and mortars. The school also taught survival and self-reliance. Men trained to cross rivers, move through swamps, climb rough ground, forage, and keep operating with little food or equipment. Coastal raids were practiced with collapsible boats, while signals training taught small patrols how to report information without exposing themselves. Graduates took these skills into the Pacific, where Independent Companies and later commando units used them for reconnaissance, raids, ambushes, and harassment operations in places such as Timor, New Guinea, and New Britain."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardDrawnFromDeck","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardSpawnedInHand","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnAfterOtherCardLeaveBoardOrOwner","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardPlayedFromHand","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardMovedToLocationInHand","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardDiscarded","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCovertCardPlayedFromHand","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartOfGame","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_3.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/GUERILLA_W